+Dual Contouring and Surface Nets are isosurface extraction methods that place a single vertex inside each grid cell intersecting the isosurface, rather than on cell edges as in Marching Cubes [G]. Dual Contouring preserves sharp features by using Hermite data (intersection points and surface normals) to minimize a Quadratic Error Function (QEF) for vertex placement [G]. Surface Nets simplifies this by abandoning gradient data, using the weighted average of edge intersections for vertex placement, resulting in faster computation and smoother topology at the cost of geometric fidelity for sharp features [G].
 ## Shared principles
-
+All methods in this family operate on a discretized domain, typically a regular grid, where each cell contains scalar values or occupancy states at its vertices or centers [G]. They generate a mesh by placing a single vertex inside each grid cell intersecting the isosurface, rather than on cell edges [G]. The vertex position is determined either by optimizing a quadratic error function using Hermite data (intersection points and normals) or by averaging edge intersection points [G].
